s** to the main verb marks it again; use base **meet**."}}},{"id":"8487c19d-372b-5bbe-a47b-2fddb531501e","topicSlug":"present-simple","level":"A1","prompt":"Every week, my friends ____ the bill at the counter. Use the present simple.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"pay"},{"id":"B","text":"pays"},{"id":"C","text":"paid"},{"id":"D","text":"paying"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Use the present simple for routines and general facts. With I, you, we and they, use the base verb. Most singular third-person subjects add -s. Focus on **affirmative** in this question.","explanation":"For the routine described, “my friends” takes pay. Use the base form with this subject.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"affirmative","contextKey":"verb-pay","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-present","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"8b18614b23e5bf0af68ae1bb06d27cf844110bf8ed2e324ac7c1657e6cb920f9","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **affirmative**. Use the present simple for routines and general facts. With I, you, we and they, use the base verb. Most singular third-person subjects add -s.","For the routine described, “my friends” takes pay. Use the base form with this subject.","Choose **pay**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"For the routine described, “my friends” takes pay. Use the base form with this subject.","B":"**pays** does not give the present-simple form for this subject. For the routine described, “my friends” takes pay. Use the base form with this subject.","C":"**paid** does not give the present-simple form for this subject. For the routine described, “my friends” takes pay. Use the base form with this subject.","D":"**paying** is the -ing form. It cannot fill the finite simple-tense or base-after-auxiliary slot requested here. For the routine described, “my friends” takes pay. Use the base form with this subject."}}},{"id":"f6c3853d-7287-51b4-aaa2-f41b7b5a5da6","topicSlug":"present-simple","level":"A1","prompt":"Complete the present-simple negative: My friends ____ pay the bill at the counter.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"did not"},{"id":"B","text":"is not"},{"id":"C","text":"do not"},{"id":"D","text":"does not"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Use do or does for questions, and do not or does not for negatives. The main verb then returns to its base form. Focus on **negative** in this question.","explanation":"Use do not with “my friends”, followed by the base form pay.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"negative","contextKey":"verb-pay","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-present","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"92e14c97746fca01df416c04540237b76d31f42f12900b44d6ae8b5e6cffd366","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **negative**. Use do or does for questions, and do not or does not for negatives. The main verb then returns to its base form.","Use do not with “my friends”, followed by the base form pay.","Choose **do not**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Did marks past time, but the question asks for present simple.","B":"Be does not form this ordinary simple-tense action-verb question or negative; use do-support.","C":"Use do not with “my friends”, followed by the base form pay.","D":"**does not** does not agree with the subject in this present-simple pattern. Use do not with “my friends”, followed by the base form pay."}}},{"id":"c5c407be-cca0-5d41-a5fb-154ab28534b1","topicSlug":"present-simple","level":"A1","prompt":"____ my friends pay the bill at the counter every week? Use the present simple.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Is"},{"id":"B","text":"Do"},{"id":"C","text":"Does"},{"id":"D","text":"Did"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Use do or does for questions, and do not or does not for negatives. The main verb then returns to its base form. Focus on **question** in this question.","explanation":"A present-simple question with “my friends” starts with do.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"question","contextKey":"verb-pay","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-present","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"9e8937dced6c13a2a2c4fee959fbd768fddbf712b1173f8a14dbc038cb3f8254","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **question**. Use do or does for questions, and do not or does not for negatives. The main verb then returns to its base form.","A present-simple question with “my friends” starts with do.","Choose **Do**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Be does not form this ordinary simple-tense action-verb question or negative; use do-support.","B":"A present-simple question with “my friends” starts with do.","C":"**Does** does not agree with the subject in this present-simple pattern. A present-simple question with “my friends” starts with do.","D":"Did marks past time, but the question asks for present simple."}}},{"id":"4c7fc602-d355-56df-a3f5-6babcc67ab35","topicSlug":"present-simple","level":"A1","prompt":"Do my friends ____ the bill at the counter regularly? (pay)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"pay"},{"id":"B","text":"pays"},{"id":"C","text":"paying"},{"id":"D","text":"to pay"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Use do or does for questions, and do not or does not for negatives. The main verb then returns to its base form. Focus on **base after auxiliary** in this question.","explanation":"After do, use the base form pay; the auxiliary carries the agreement.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"base-after-auxiliary","contextKey":"verb-pay","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-present","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"e47d4b81eed69721195d90394f5b2f222bbfd99acddde0ecab93f48d7e04051b","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **base after auxiliary**. Use do or does for questions, and do not or does not for negatives. The main verb then returns to its base form.","After do, use the base form pay; the auxiliary carries the agreement.","Choose **pay**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"After do, use the base form pay; the auxiliary carries the agreement.","B":"The auxiliary already carries tense/agreement. Adding **-s/-es** to the main verb marks it again; use base **pay**.","C":"**paying** is the -ing form. It cannot fill the finite simple-tense or base-after-auxiliary slot requested here. After do, use the base form pay; the auxiliary carries the agreement.","D":"This slot does not take a **to-infinitive**. After do, use the base form pay; the auxiliary carries the agreement."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["The present simple presents a habit, a general fact or a situation viewed as stable. It does not always mean that an action is happening this second. In She works at a café, the job is the relevant fact; she may be at home right now. Look at both the time meaning and the subject before choosing a form."],"rules":[{"title":"Habits and facts","explanation":"Use the present simple for routines and general facts. With I, you, we and they, use the base verb. Most singular third-person subjects add -s.","examples":["We cycle to college.","The guide explains the route.","The guide explain**s** the route every morning."],"pattern":"I **work** / She work**s**","paragraphs":["The ending **-s is added to the verb**, not to the subject. It appears with third-person singular subjects such as he, she, it and the driver. I and you do not take it. A plural noun subject also uses the base verb: The drivers work."]},{"title":"Spelling","explanation":"Some verbs add -es. A consonant followed by y usually changes to -ies; a vowel followed by y keeps the y.","examples":["She carries the equipment.","He plays chess.","**Does** the guide **explain** the route?"],"pattern":"watch → watch**es** · study → stud**ies** · play → play**s**","paragraphs":["Learn spelling with the verb. After a consonant + y, change y to -ies: carry → carries. After a vowel + y, keep y: enjoy → enjoys. Go → goes and do → does add -es; have → has is irregular. The pronunciation of the ending also varies."]},{"title":"Do and does","explanation":"Use do or does for questions, and do not or does not for negatives. The main verb then returns to its base form.","examples":["Does the driver stop here?","We do not work on Sundays.","The guide **doesn’t explain** the route on Sundays."],"pattern":"**Does** she **work**? / She **does not work**.","paragraphs":["In a question or negative, the auxiliary do/does carries the agreement. That is why Does she works? is incorrect: it marks agreement twice. Keep the main verb at its base: Does she work? The same principle applies after doesn’t."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Frequency adverbs normally precede the lexical verb: she usually works. Present simple also describes published schedules: The train leaves at six. Stative verbs such as know commonly use simple rather than continuous forms. Present simple is not restricted to repeated visible actions.","examples":["The guide explain**s** the route every morning.","**Does** the guide **explain** the route?","The guide **doesn’t explain** the route on Sundays."]}],"recap":["I **work** / She work**s**","watch → watch**es** · study → stud**ies** · play → play**s**","**Does** she **work**? / She **does not work**."]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["have-got"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
